isotopes we can practically use as projectiles or targets are all relatively proton‑rich (e.g., calcium‑48, lead‑208, bismuth‑209). When we fuse them, the resulting compound nucleus always has far too few neutrons. For example, the reaction that made element 118 used calcium‑48 (20 protons, 28 neutrons) and californium‑249 (98 protons, 151 neutrons) to produce oganesson‑294 (118 protons, only 176 neutrons)—still 8 neutrons short of the magic 184. As a result, the actual isotopes we create are severely neutron‑deficient and fall apart instantly via fission or alpha decay. No matter how cleverly we “know” the promised land of the island, we can’t swim there using only the stable‑isotope building blocks we have. ### 3. Radioactive beams aren’t yet intense enough The obvious solution is to use a beam or target that is itself radioactive and neutron‑rich—like a beam of calcium‑50 or tin‑132. In theory, that could push the final nucleus closer to the island. In practice, producing an intense, stable, focused beam of short‑lived exotic nuclei is a monumental technical challenge. Radioactive‑ion‑beam facilities are growing, but their intensity is still orders of magnitude too low to compensate for the already minuscule fusion probability. So far, no superheavy element has been discovered using such beams. ### 4. The properties we “know” aren’t really known You mention “we know the properties of” new elements. In reality, we only have theoretical predictions, often varying wildly between different nuclear models. For instance, estimates of the half-life for element 126 with 184 neutrons range from a few seconds to millions of years. Without actual synthesis experiments, we can’t confirm which model is right. The very reason we want to make these elements is to test those predictions—not because we already know the answer well enough to skip the experiment. ### 5. The engineering limits of detection Even if a long‑lived superheavy nucleus were produced, catching it among tril